{
  "title": "Distance at Constant Speed",
  "scope": "Understanding the direct proportionality between distance, speed, and time under constant speed conditions.",
  "objectives": [
    {
      "id": "o1",
      "label": "Calculate distance from speed and time",
      "criterion": "The learner correctly applies the formula distance = speed \u00D7 time using matching units to compute travel distance.",
      "sourceIds": ["s1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "o2",
      "label": "Analyze proportional scaling",
      "criterion": "The learner explains and predicts how doubling speed or time affects the resulting distance.",
      "sourceIds": ["s1"]
    }
  ],
  "knowledge": "When an object moves at a constant speed, its motion is governed by the relationship distance = speed \u00D7 time. This equation shows that distance is directly proportional to both speed and time. If time is doubled while speed remains constant, the distance traveled also doubles. Similarly, if speed is doubled while time is held constant, the distance increases by a factor of two. To ensure accurate calculations, dimensional consistency is essential: multiplying a speed expressed in metres per second ($\u005Cpu{m/s}$) by a time interval in seconds ($\u005Cpu{s}$) yields a distance in metres ($\u005Cpu{m}$).",
  "concerns": [],
  "misconception": "Adding speed and time together instead of multiplying them to find distance.",
  "misconceptionCorrection": "Remind yourself that speed measures how fast distance accumulates every second. To find the total distance, you must scale that rate by the total duration through multiplication ($\u005Ctext{distance} = \u005Ctext{speed} \u00D7 \u005Ctext{time}$), not addition.",
  "opening": "I am really curious how an object's speed and travel time combine to determine how far it goes. Could you please teach me how to calculate distance at a constant speed?",
  "transfer": {
    "question": "A train travels along a straight track at a constant speed of \u005Cpu{25 m/s} for a duration of \u005Cpu{120 s}. Determine the total distance covered by the train.",
    "expected": "The total distance is calculated by multiplying the constant speed by the elapsed time: distance = speed \u00D7 time = \u005Cpu{25 m/s} \u00D7 \u005Cpu{120 s} = {{distance}} \u005Cpu{m}.",
    "criteria": [
      "Identifies the correct formula distance = speed \u00D7 time.",
      "Multiplies speed of \u005Cpu{25 m/s} by time of \u005Cpu{120 s}.",
      "Arrives at the correct numerical distance of \u005Cpu{3000 m} with proper units."
    ],
    "numeric": {
      "fields": [
        {
          "id": "distance",
          "label": "Total Distance",
          "unit": "m",
          "expected": 3000,
          "tolerance": 0
        }
      ],
      "inputs": [
        {
          "id": "speed",
          "value": 25
        },
        {
          "id": "time",
          "value": 120
        }
      ],
      "code": "function calculate(inputs) { const distance = inputs.speed * inputs.time; return { metrics: { distance: distance } }; }"
    }
  },
